  m_Name: DrumBrakeData
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  engineName: Drum Brake
  engineDescription: 'A Drum Brake is a mechanical braking system used to slow down
    or stop the rotation of a wheel by creating friction between brake shoes and
    the inner surface of a rotating brake drum. Its main components include the brake
    drum, brake shoes, wheel cylinder, return springs, backing plate, adjuster mechanism,
    and parking brake lever. During operation, hydraulic pressure or mechanical force
    pushes the brake shoes outward against the inner surface of the rotating drum,
    generating friction that converts the vehicle''s kinetic energy into heat, thereby
    reducing the wheel''s speed. When the braking force is released, the return springs
    retract the brake shoes to their original position.


    Drum brakes are widely
    used in passenger vehicles, commercial vehicles, motorcycles, and heavy-duty
    equipment, particularly on rear wheels due to their durability, cost-effectiveness,
    and integrated parking brake functionality.'
  engineCategory: Engineering
